#align(center)[= InkyCap Documentation  ]
#align(center)[#image("/Assets/inkycap-logo.svg", width: 20%, alt: "Otto — the InkyCap logo")]
Welcome to InkyCap: a personal knowledge management (PKM) application for academics, researchers, and writers of all kinds. You write in #link("https://typst.app")[Typst]'s light, readable markup while InkyCap links your notes together, keeps your metadata queryable, your citations formatted, and turns your work into beautifully typeset PDFs, books, and web pages.

This manual is itself an InkyCap notebox, so every page you read is also a working example of its markup usage, wikilinks, and the formats that it describes.

Read #wikilink("4 - Quick Start") if you want to explore on your own. It takes you from a fresh installation to a linked note in a few minutes. Otherwise, refer to the sections below or use the search button on the left sidebar to find something.


== The Manual

=== #wikilink("1 - Getting Started", display: "Getting Started")
- Install InkyCap, create your first notebox, write a note, and make your first link.

=== #wikilink("1 - The InkyCap Interface", display: "InkyCap Interface")
- A guided tour of the window (toolbar, sidebars, tabs, command palette, and settings).

=== #wikilink("1 - Writing and Editing", display: "Writing and Editing")
- Writing, formatting, linking, tagging, properties, and citations. These make up the everyday core.

=== #wikilink("1 - Views and Navigation", display: "Views and Navigation")
- See your notes by time (Journal Scroll), by connection (Mycelial View), and by task (Agenda).

=== #wikilink("1 - Collections and Publishing", display: "Collections and Publishing")
- Treat notes as a database, and export them as spreadsheets, PDFs, books, and web pages.

=== #wikilink("1 - Collaboration", display: "Collaboration")
 - Share a notebox with others through git sync or an offline package exchange.

=== #wikilink("1 - Importing and Extending", display: "Importing and Extending")
- Import from Markdown and Obsidian, set up scaffolds and templates, and add extensions.
